steps.body:
A feed handler and a strategy, two programs on one machine, need to pass messages. A **socket** is an endpoint for that, and like a file it's a file descriptor. The kernel holds the bytes in flight: you write into one end and read from the other. `socketpair` from `<sys/socket.h>` makes two connected ends in one call, the simplest real socket:

```cpp harness
#include <sys/socket.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int main() {
    {{code}}
    close(fds[0]);
    close(fds[1]);
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
int fds[2];
socketpair(AF_UNIX, SOCK_STREAM, 0, fds);  // AF_UNIX: within this machine
send(fds[0], "PING", 4, 0);
char buf[16];
const ssize_t n = recv(fds[1], buf, sizeof buf, 0);
std::cout << n << ' ' << std::string(buf, n) << '\n';  // 4 PING
```

`send` copies bytes into the kernel and returns how many it took. `recv` copies out what has arrived, at most your buffer's size, and waits while nothing has. Both return a `ssize_t`, POSIX's signed size, like `write` in *Processes and System Calls*, and `std::string(buf, n)` makes a string of the `n` bytes that came. Both return -1 on an error and set `errno`, a global from `<cerrno>` that says why. `recv` returns 0 once the other end is closed and everything is read, and `close` gives a descriptor back.

The second argument picks one of two kinds of socket, and the difference shapes every protocol:

- `SOCK_STREAM` carries a stream of bytes with no boundaries. Two sends can arrive in one `recv`, and one send can be split across two. So the bytes need **framing**, like a `'\n'` after each message or its length in front.
- `SOCK_DGRAM` carries **datagrams**. Each send is one message, and each `recv` returns exactly one, whole. A buffer too small for it gets the start, and the rest of that message is thrown away.

Across a network, TCP is the stream kind and UDP is the datagram kind.

example.code:
#include <sys/socket.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

void send_text(int fd, const std::string& text) {
    send(fd, text.data(), text.size(), 0);
}

// One recv: whatever the kernel has, up to 64 bytes.
std::string recv_once(int fd) {
    char buf[64];
    const ssize_t n = recv(fd, buf, sizeof buf, 0);
    return n > 0 ? std::string(buf, n) : std::string();
}

int main() {
    int stream[2];
    socketpair(AF_UNIX, SOCK_STREAM, 0, stream);  // two connected ends
    send_text(stream[0], "BUY 100\n");
    send_text(stream[0], "SELL 40\n");
    std::string bytes = recv_once(stream[1]);
    std::cout << "stream: one recv got " << bytes.size() << " bytes\n";
    std::size_t nl;
    while ((nl = bytes.find('\n')) != std::string::npos) {  // frame by '\n'
        std::cout << "  message: " << bytes.substr(0, nl) << '\n';
        bytes.erase(0, nl + 1);
    }

    int dgram[2];
    socketpair(AF_UNIX, SOCK_DGRAM, 0, dgram);
    send_text(dgram[0], "BUY 100");
    send_text(dgram[0], "SELL 40");
    std::cout << "datagram: first recv got \"" << recv_once(dgram[1]) << "\"\n";
    std::cout << "datagram: second recv got \"" << recv_once(dgram[1]) << "\"\n";

    for (int fd : {stream[0], stream[1], dgram[0], dgram[1]}) {
        close(fd);
    }
    return 0;
}

