steps.body:
Orders go to an exchange over TCP, since an order that's lost, or arrives twice, is never acceptable. **TCP** gives two sockets a **connection**, a reliable stream of bytes. The kernel numbers every byte, the receiver acknowledges what it got, and anything lost is sent again, so your program sees every byte once and in order. That costs time: a connection opens with a handshake of three packets (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK), and one lost packet holds up everything behind it until it's resent.

The two sides set up differently:

- The server makes a `SOCK_STREAM` socket, `bind`s it to an address, and calls `listen`. From then on the kernel completes connections into a queue, and `accept` takes the next one off it as a new descriptor, one per client.
- The client makes a socket and calls `connect` with the server's address.

TCP may also hold back a small write for a moment, hoping to send it along with the next one, which is called **Nagle's algorithm**. That's right for bulk data and wrong for an order, so trading code turns it off with `setsockopt` and `TCP_NODELAY`:

```cpp
#include <netinet/in.h>
#include <netinet/tcp.h>
#include <sys/socket.h>

const int fd = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM, 0);
int on = 1;
std::cout << setsockopt(fd, IPPROTO_TCP, TCP_NODELAY, &on, sizeof on) << '\n';  // 0
```

One program can play both sides with a thread. `std::thread` from `<thread>` starts running a lambda as soon as it's built, at the same time as the code after it, and `join()` waits for it to finish. *Threads, Data Races, and Mutexes*, in the Concurrency section, covers threads in full. Here only the client prints, so the output never depends on which thread runs first.

TCP is a byte stream, so its messages need framing, as on a stream socket pair. And `send` can take fewer bytes than you gave it when the kernel's buffer is full, so real code loops until every byte is sent.

example.code:
#include <arpa/inet.h>
#include <netinet/in.h>
#include <netinet/tcp.h>
#include <sys/socket.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <thread>

void send_line(int fd, const std::string& text) {
    const std::string line = text + '\n';
    send(fd, line.data(), line.size(), 0);
}

// Reads up to the next '\n', keeping extra bytes in pending. False once the peer closed.
bool read_line(int fd, std::string& pending, std::string& line) {
    std::size_t nl;
    while ((nl = pending.find('\n')) == std::string::npos) {
        char buf[256];
        const ssize_t n = recv(fd, buf, sizeof buf, 0);
        if (n <= 0) return false;
        pending.append(buf, n);
    }
    line = pending.substr(0, nl);
    pending.erase(0, nl + 1);
    return true;
}

int main() {
    const int listener = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM, 0);
    sockaddr_in addr{};
    addr.sin_family = AF_INET;
    addr.sin_port = htons(0);
    inet_pton(AF_INET, "127.0.0.1", &addr.sin_addr);
    bind(listener, reinterpret_cast<sockaddr*>(&addr), sizeof addr);
    listen(listener, 8);  // from here, connections wait in a queue until accepted
    socklen_t len = sizeof addr;
    getsockname(listener, reinterpret_cast<sockaddr*>(&addr), &len);

    const int client = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM, 0);
    if (connect(client, reinterpret_cast<sockaddr*>(&addr), sizeof addr) != 0) {
        std::cout << "connect failed\n";
        return 1;
    }  // connected already: the kernel queued it, and accept will take it

    std::thread gateway([listener] {  // the exchange's side, running at the same time
        const int conn = accept(listener, nullptr, nullptr);
        std::string pending, line;
        while (read_line(conn, pending, line)) {
            send_line(conn, "ACK " + line);  // echo each order back
        }
        close(conn);
    });

    int on = 1;
    setsockopt(client, IPPROTO_TCP, TCP_NODELAY, &on, sizeof on);  // send small messages at once
    send_line(client, "NEW 1 AAPL B 100");
    send_line(client, "NEW 2 MSFT S 40");
    send_line(client, "NEW 3 AAPL S 100");
    std::string pending, reply;
    for (int i = 0; i < 3 && read_line(client, pending, reply); ++i) {
        std::cout << reply << '\n';
    }
    close(client);  // the gateway's read_line sees the close and returns false
    gateway.join();
    close(listener);
    return 0;
}

